(defmodule liveeval
  "The **live-evaluating buffer**, as a vocabulary — the half of \"type Brood and watch it
run\" that has nothing to do with what the buffer is *for*.

Two features want it. The tutorial (`tutor`) evaluates the boxes of a lesson and grades them
against an expected answer; the playground (`playground`) evaluates a whole free-text file
and reports what came back. Everything BETWEEN those two — deciding whether a half-typed
form is an error or just unfinished, saying how long something took, saying what it left
running, rendering a spy cascade — is the same work, and was written once in the tutorial
where only the tutorial could reach it.

What lives here is exactly what both need and neither owns:

  [[parse-state]]    is this text blank, mid-form, broken, or ready to send?
  [[slow-note]]      how long it took, once that is worth knowing
  [[proc-note]]      what it left running (concurrency has no return value to look at)
  [[result-note]]    the `=> value` line, with `printed:` when it printed
  [[type-hint]]      the `: type` / `name : sig` hint that belongs at the form itself
  [[error-note]]     the `✗ …` line for a raise or a timeout
  [[spy-lines]]      spy entries → an indented call/return cascade
  [[report-lines]]   the WHOLE reply as a pane's body — value, printed, cost, cascade

All pure: a string or a decoded reply map in, a string or a note map out. No model, no
buffer, no sandbox — which is what makes it testable without any of them, and what stops
this from quietly becoming a third feature.

**The note shape** every caller stamps through `results/eol-notes`: `{:kind :text}` where
`:kind` is `:info` (a result), `:pass`, `:fail`, or `:pending` (mid-typing — dim, never a
verdict colour, because a form you are still writing is not a mistake), plus `:error?` on a
note that means \"it ran and blew up\" as opposed to \"it ran and was wrong\".

A result note may also carry **`:hint`** — [[type-hint]]'s answer, travelling with the value
but placed apart from it by `results/set-note`, which anchors the answer below the form and
the type at it.")
;; No imports at all, deliberately. `eval-server/*spy-entry-cap*`, `reflect/expr-type` and
;; `reflect/source-signatures` are referenced qualified below (each auto-loads); everything
;; else is prelude. A module that needed the model would be a third feature rather than the
;; vocabulary two features share.

(def *slow-ms* 150) ; past this, a result shows how long it took
;; ---- is this text ready to evaluate? -------------------------------------------

(defn parse-state (src)
  "Classify source text for a live evaluator: `:blank`, `[:incomplete reason]` (mid-typing),
`[:err msg]` (broken, and more typing won't fix it), or `:ok`.

The distinction that matters is the middle one. E0002 is the reader saying \"this could still
become valid\" — an unclosed list, an unterminated string — and that is the state EVERY form
passes through on the way to being typed. It must not read as a mistake; it must not read as
NOTHING either, since silence there is what makes a stale result look current. So the reason
travels with the state (\"unterminated string\") and callers show it in the pending voice.
E0001 (\"unexpected ')'\") is a real error — no further typing fixes it — and reports as one."
  (cond
    (= (string/trim src) "") :blank
    else
      (try
        (do (reflect/read-all src) :ok)
        (catch e
          (if (and (map? e) (= (:code e) "E0002"))
            [:incomplete (error-message e)]
            [:err (error-message e)])))))

;; ---- what a reply is worth saying about ----------------------------------------

(defn slow-note (reply)
  "`\"  (312ms)\"` once an evaluation is slow enough to have been felt, else `\"\"`."
  (let (ms (get reply :ms 0))
    (if (>= ms *slow-ms*) (str "  (" ms "ms)") "")))

(defn proc-note (reply)
  "`\"  ⚙ 2 processes still running\"`, or `\"\"` when nothing survived the evaluation.

Concurrency has no return value to look at, so what an evaluation LEFT RUNNING (`:procs`
from the eval server) is the only thing that reports it. Silent when nothing did, so it
only speaks up where processes are actually the point."
  (let (alive (get-in reply [:procs :alive] 0))
    (cond
      (= alive 0) ""
      (= alive 1) "  ⚙ 1 process still running"
      else (str "  ⚙ " alive " processes still running"))))

(defn printed-note (reply)
  "`\"   (printed: hi)\"` when the evaluation wrote to stdout, else `\"\"` — the half of a
REPL that a value alone loses."
  (if (= (get reply :output "") "")
    ""
    (str "   (printed: " (string/trim (get reply :output)) ")")))

(defn defined-hint (src)
  "`\"foo : (int -> int)\"` — what the first form of `src` DEFINES and how the checker types
it, or nil when it defines nothing (or defines something the type grammar cannot write).

The gap this closes: a `(defn foo …)` form evaluates to its own name, so a live evaluator
has `=> foo` to show and nothing else — and `foo` is a value, not a type. `expr-type` cannot
help, because the type of that form's VALUE is the type of a symbol. `source-signatures`
asks the checker the question actually being asked: what did this text define, and what is
it typed as?"
  (let (sigs (try (reflect/source-signatures src) (catch _ nil))
        hit (seq/find (fn (entry) (string? (:sig entry))) (or sigs '())))
    (when hit (str (:name hit) " : " (:sig hit)))))

(defn expression-hint (src)
  "`\": int\"` — the type the advisory checker infers for the first form of `src`, or nil
when it has no opinion (an anonymous `fn`, an unparsable fragment).

`reflect/expr-type` is the primitive this reads; it exists because the checker computes a
type for every expression it walks and nothing could ask it for one."
  (let (t (try (reflect/expr-type src) (catch _ nil)))
    (when t (str ": " t))))

(defn type-hint (src)
  "The derived-type hint for a form: its signature when it defines something, else the type
of the value it evaluates to, else nil.

Shown at the form itself rather than beside its result, and that is the point of separating
it from [[result-note]]: the value is what the code DID and the type is what the language
thinks it MAY do, so the two belong at different places — the type at the code it describes,
the answer under it. A definition had nothing to show at all before, which is the case that
forced the split."
  (or (defined-hint src) (expression-hint src)))

(defn error-note (reply)
  "The `✗ …` note for a reply that raised or timed out, or nil when it did neither.

`:error?` marks \"the code ran and blew up\" as distinct from \"it ran and was wrong\" — a
caller pairing this with a checker advisory wants the advisory on the second and not the
first, where it would only repeat the error."
  (cond
    (get reply :timeout)
      {:kind :fail
       :error? true
       :text "✗ timed out — still running? (C-c C-k restarts the sandbox)"}
    (not (get reply :ok)) {:kind :fail :error? true :text (str "✗ " (get reply :error "error"))}
    else nil))

(defn result-note (reply &optional (src nil))
  "The plain-result note for a successful reply: `=> 6`, plus what it printed, how long it
took and what it left running. With `src`, the note also carries `:hint` — the form's derived
type ([[type-hint]]), which a caller anchors at the FORM rather than here, so a type appearing
or changing never moves the answer. Errors and timeouts are [[error-note]]'s."
  (or (error-note reply)
    (let (hint (when src (type-hint src))
          note {:kind :info
                :text (str "=> "
                        (get reply :value)
                        (printed-note reply)
                        (slow-note reply)
                        (proc-note reply))})
      (if hint (assoc note :hint hint) note))))

;; ---- the spy cascade ------------------------------------------------------------

(defn- strip-parens
  (s)
  ;; "(5)" -> "5" — an args list reads better without its wrapping parens
  (if (and (string? s) (string/starts-with? s "(") (string/ends-with? s ")"))
    (string/substring s 1 (dec (string/length s)))
    (or s "")))

(defn- indent
  (lines)
  ;; two spaces, the one nesting step this vocabulary uses
  (map (fn (l) (str "  " l)) lines))

(defn spy-lines (entries)
  "Spy entries → indented lines: a `call` opens a level (`fac 3`), its `return` closes it
(`= 6`); anything else renders flat."
  (let (r (fold (fn (acc e)
                  (let ([depth ls] acc
                        kind (:kind e))
                    (cond
                      (= kind "call")
                        [(inc depth)
                          (cons (str (string/repeat "  " depth)
                                  (:fn e)
                                  " "
                                  (strip-parens (:args e)))
                            ls)]
                      (= kind "return")
                        (let (d (math/max 0 (dec depth)))
                          [d (cons (str (string/repeat "  " d) "= " (:value e)) ls)])
                      else [depth (cons (str (:form e) " => " (:value e)) ls)])))
            [0 '()]
            entries))
    (reverse (second r))))

(defn budget-spent? (entries)
  "Whether this cascade is all the sandbox would collect — it stopped at the trace budget
(`eval-server/*spy-entry-cap*`) rather than because the code stopped calling.

Worth telling the reader, and not a footnote: the loop kept running after this, in constant
stack and at full speed, because the tracer steps aside once its sink is full
(`debug/trace-fn`). A pane claiming to show \"every call\" would be lying."
  (>= (count entries) eval-server/*spy-entry-cap*))

(def *untraced-note*
  ;; One dim line, last in the report — see [[trace-lines]].
  "· nothing of yours ran; the trace follows what this session defines")

(defn trace-lines (reply)
  "How the evaluation got where it did: the `:spy` cascade under a line naming it, with the
trace-budget caveat when it is a truncation rather than the whole story.

Two cases where an EMPTY trace is not just an empty section:

  - **It raised, and traced nothing.** No line at all — `✗ …` is the story, and \"nothing of
    yours ran\" beside it is true and unhelpful. (A cascade IS shown for a raise: how far it
    got before it blew up is exactly what the pane is for.)
  - **It ran fine and traced nothing.** One dim line, [[*untraced-note*]], and last — because
    \"traced nothing\" and \"nothing happened\" look identical and the difference is worth one
    line, but only one. It began as a paragraph explaining the whole tracing policy, which
    was right when it was the only thing the pane could say and became a wall the moment the
    pane had a value, an output and a cost to show above it."
  (let (entries (or (:spy reply) ()))
    (cond
      (not (empty? entries))
        (append (list (if (budget-spent? entries)
                        (str "the first " (count entries) " traced calls, innermost nested:")
                        "every traced call and return, innermost nested:"))
          (indent (spy-lines entries))
          (if (budget-spent? entries)
            (list "" "…the trace budget stops here; the code carried on untraced.")
            '()))
      (error-note reply) '()
      else (list *untraced-note*))))

(defn result-lines (reply &optional (src nil))
  "What the evaluation ANSWERED — `=> 42` with its derived type beside it, or the `✗ …` it
raised. Never empty: every reply answered something, even when the answer is a failure."
  (let (err (error-note reply))
    (if err
      (list (:text err))
      (let (hint (when src (type-hint src)))
        (list (str "=> " (get reply :value) (if hint (str "   " hint) "")))))))

(defn printed-lines (reply)
  "What the evaluation PRINTED, verbatim and in full — `()` when it printed nothing.

The half a one-line note structurally cannot show. [[printed-note]] squeezes the output onto
the end of the result line, trimmed to a single run-on and then clipped by the pane width, so
three printed lines arrive as one and a long one arrives cut. Here there is room for it as it
was written, which for a program whose output IS the point is the difference between a
report and a rumour."
  (let (out (string/trimr (get reply :output "")))
    (if (= (string/trim out) "")
      '()
      (append (list "printed:") (indent (string/split out "\n"))))))

(defn cost-lines (reply)
  "What the evaluation COST — how long it took once that is worth knowing, and what it left
running. `()` when it was quick and left nothing behind, so the pane stays quiet about the
ordinary case."
  (let (parts (append (if (>= (get reply :ms 0) *slow-ms*) (list (str (get reply :ms) "ms")) '())
                (let (alive (get-in reply [:procs :alive] 0))
                  (cond
                    (= alive 0) '()
                    (= alive 1) (list "1 process still running")
                    else (list (str alive " processes still running"))))))
    (if (empty? parts) '() (list (string/join " · " parts)))))

(defn report-lines (reply &optional (src nil))
  "Everything one reply says, as a pane's body lines: what it answered, what it printed, what
it cost, and how it got there — each section separated by a blank line and each silent when
it has nothing to add.

The pane's counterpart to [[result-note]], which says the same things in one line's worth of
room. They deliberately overlap on the value: the note is what you read while typing, this is
what you read when the note was not enough."
  (let (section (fn (acc more) (if (empty? more) acc (append acc (list "") more))))
    (section (section (section (result-lines reply src) (printed-lines reply))
               (cost-lines reply))
      (trace-lines reply))))

(defn report-text (reply &optional (src nil))
  "[[report-lines]] as one string — a pane's body."
  (string/join "\n" (report-lines reply src)))
